4️⃣ Capacity logic (exactly what you asked)

Step A — Load & reshape

import pandas as pd

df = pd.read_excel("capacity.xlsx")

week_cols = df.columns[2:]

long_df = df.melt(
    id_vars=["Project", "Person"],
    value_vars=week_cols,
    var_name="Week",
    value_name="Engaged"
)

long_df["Week"] = pd.to_datetime(long_df["Week"])


⸻

Step B — Define new project window

start_week = pd.to_datetime("2024-03-04")
end_week   = pd.to_datetime("2024-04-15")


⸻

Step C — Compute engagement per person

window_df = long_df[
    (long_df["Week"] >= start_week) &
    (long_df["Week"] <= end_week)
]

capacity = (
    window_df
    .groupby("Person")["Engaged"]
    .sum()
    .reset_index()
)

capacity["Free_Weeks"] = (
    window_df["Week"].nunique() - capacity["Engaged"]
)

capacity = capacity.sort_values("Engaged")

✅ This gives you exactly what you want:
	•	People sorted from least engaged → most engaged
	•	Who has continuous free windows
	•	Who is overloaded

⸻

5️⃣ Detect “empty slot” (continuous availability)

This is where Python beats Power BI.

def has_free_slot(df, start, end):
    weeks = pd.date_range(start, end, freq="7D")
    status = df.set_index("Week").reindex(weeks, fill_value=0)
    return status["Engaged"].sum() == 0

availability = (
    long_df.groupby("Person")
    .apply(has_free_slot, start_week, end_week)
    .reset_index(name="Fully_Free")
)

Now you can answer:

“Who is completely free between X and Y?”

